parts.text:
The user is saying that after adding advanced (sections) rules, the first setting (the file-level settings like color mode, print style, etc.) is being skipped/ignored. Only the sections' settings are used.

Let me think about what's happening:

In `createPrintJobsForFile` in server.js:
```js
const s = file.settings || {};
const sections = s.sections || [];

if (sections.length > 0) {
    const jobs = [];
    for (const [index, section] of sections.entries()) {
        const sectionPages = [];
        for (let p = (section.startPage || 1) - 1; p < (section.endPage || file.pageCount); p++) {
            sectionPages.push(p);
        }
        const sectionPagesPerSheet = section.pagesPerSheet || s.pagesPerSheet || 1;
        const job = {
            ...
            colorMode: section.colorMode || s.colorMode || 'auto',
            printStyle: section.printStyle || s.printStyle || 'single',
            ...
            copies: section.copies || s.copies || 1,
            ...
        };
        ...
    }
    return jobs;
}
```

When sections exist, it creates one print job per section. Each section's settings override the file-level settings. If a section doesn't specify a setting (e.g., colorMode), it falls back to the file-level setting.

So if the user:
1. Sets file-level: colorMode='color', printStyle='single', copies=3, N-up=2
2. Adds sections with just startPage/endPage (no colorMode specified)

Then each section should inherit colorMode='color', printStyle='single', copies=3, N-up=2 from the file-level settings.

But wait - the `addSection` function adds sections with default values:
```js
colorMode: 'bw',  // hardcoded default, NOT from file-level settings
printStyle: 'single',  // hardcoded default, NOT from file-level settings
pagesPerSheet: 1,  // hardcoded default, NOT from file-level settings
copies: 1,  // hardcoded default, NOT from file-level settings
```

When the section has `colorMode: 'bw'` (hardcoded default), the OR `section.colorMode || s.colorMode || 'auto'` evaluates to `section.colorMode` which is `'bw'`, IGNORING the file-level setting.

So the fix is: when creating a new section, DON'T hardcode defaults. Instead, inherit from the file-level settings:

```js
const current = getSettings(fileIndex);
updateSetting(fileIndex, 'sections', [...sections, {
    id: Date.now(),
    startPage,
    endPage: startPage,
    paperSize: current.paperSize || 'A4',
    colorMode: current.colorMode || 'bw',
    printStyle: current.printStyle || 'single',
    copies: current.copies || 1,
    pagesPerSheet: current.pagesPerSheet || 1,
}]);
```

By using `current.colorMode` instead of `'bw'`, the section inherits the file-level color mode.

Also, in the backend `createPrintJobsForFile`, the fallback chain is:
```js
const job = {
