parts.text:
Now write a Node CDP driver script using Node's native WebSocket to:
1. Evaluate `document.body.innerText` — check Activation screen
2. Fill fields and click Activate

The Activation screen has TextFields (MUI). Filling MUI inputs via JS: I can set the native input value and dispatch input events. Simpler: evaluate JS that finds input elements by placeholder/label. MUI TextField renders `<input>` with placeholder (from placeholder prop) or via label (aria-labelledby). I'll query inputs: the Server URL input has placeholder "https://patel-autoprint.onrender.com", Activation Key input has placeholder "PATEL-XXXXXXXX", Machine Name input — no placeholder, label "Machine Name".

Plan in page:
```js
const inputs = document.querySelectorAll('input');
// inputs[0] server url, inputs[1] activation key, inputs[2] machine name
const setter = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(window.HTMLInputElement.prototype, 'value').set;
setter.call(inputs[0], 'http://localhost:5000');
inputs[0].dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
setter.call(inputs[1], 'PATEL-0B388397');
inputs[1].dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
```
Then click the Activate button: `[...document.querySelectorAll('button')].find(b => b.textContent.includes('Activate')).click();`

Then wait and read body.innerText to see if we get the Layout (Print Queue).

Note: activationKey is stored via persist (zustand persist to localStorage) with `saveSettings()` which writes to window.localStorage and also does IPC? Let me check saveSettings — it might call electron store. Fine either way.

Let me write the script.

