Confirmed: clicking "Update & Restart" calls `window.patelApp.updates.install()` → IPC `app:install-update` → `autoUpdater.quitAndInstall()`.

Now let me implement the permanent fixes in main.js:

1. **Single instance lock** at the top (before app.whenReady):
```js
const gotTheLock = app.requestSingleInstanceLock();
if (!gotTheLock) {
  app.quit();
} else {
  app.on('second-instance', () => {
    if (mainWindow) {
      if (mainWindow.isMinimized()) mainWindow.restore();
      mainWindow.show();
      mainWindow.focus();
    }
  });
}
```
But the rest of the code (createWindow, etc.) is at module scope. If lock not acquired, app.quit() then the module still runs setupAutoUpdater etc. — but app quits before whenReady resolves? app.quit() prevents whenReady from... Actually app.quit() will still let whenReady fire? In Electron, calling app.quit() before ready is allowed; the 'ready' event won't fire / the app exits. To be safe, structure: if lock not acquired → app.quit() and don't run the rest. Common pattern:

```js
const gotTheLock = app.requestSingleInstanceLock();
if (!gotTheLock) {
  app.quit();
} else {
  // ... rest of app setup (all the code below)
}
```

That requires wrapping the whole file — big restructure. Alternative simpler pattern used widely:

```js
const gotTheLock = app.requestSingleInstanceLock();
if (!gotTheLock) {
  app.quit();
  // stop execution
} else {
  app.on('second-instance', () => { ... });
}
```
and keep the rest — since when the lock isn't got, app.quit() will terminate; the subsequent `whenReady().then(...)` may not run. But `setupAutoUpdater` is only called in whenReady. The top-level code creates `agent`, `whatsapp` objects — harmless. `createWindow()` is only called in whenReady. So the only thing running is the whenReady chain. If lock not acquired, app.quit() → 'ready' may still fire in some versions?? 

Actually in Electron, if you call `app.quit()` before 'ready', the app exits without emitting 'ready' (whenReady never resolves). This is the documented standard pattern. So the `else` isn't even strictly needed, but the second-instance handler must be registered regardless. Standard pattern:

```js
if (!app.requestSingleInstanceLock()) {
  app.quit();
} else {
  app.on('second-instance', () => {
    if (mainWindow) { ... focus ... }
  });
}
```
And whenReady continues below. Good — minimal change, keeps everything at module scope. The whenReady code won't run in the second instance because app.quit() was called pre-ready.

2. **Hard-exit after spawning installer**:
```js
ipcMain.handle('app:install-update', () => {
  if (isDev) return;
  isQuitting = true;
  autoUpdater.quitAndInstall(true, true);
  setTimeout(() => {
    app.exit(0);
  }, 250);
});
```
Using isSilent=true avoids any UAC-ish prompts, and isForceRunAfter=true relaunches the app after install. The 250ms delay gives the detached installer time to spawn (it spawns synchronously inside quitAndInstall, so even 0ms would work; but a small delay is belt-and-braces in case the async install path is used). Then app.exit(0) guarantees our process dies before NSIS's process check.

Wait, but one concern: if I call app.exit(0) 250ms later, and during that window the user's app is still visible... 250ms is imperceptible. Good.

Actually, even better: I realize there might be an issue with isForceRunAfter + the NSIS install directory. Fine.

3. Also — should I disable `autoInstallOnAppQuit`? No — it works fine (install happens onQuit, after process is dead). Keep it.

4. Consider: with single-instance lock, if the user clicks the shortcut while app is running, second-instance focuses existing. Good.

Now, there's also a subtle issue with the update-downloaded flow across instances: each instance checks for updates. With single lock, only one runs. Good.

Let me implement. First edit main.js.

Edit 1: add single-instance lock after `isDev` const and before `let mainWindow`.

Current code:
```js
const isDev = !app.isPackaged;

let mainWindow = null;
let tray = null;
let isQuitting = false;
```

Replace with:
```js
const isDev = !app.isPackaged;

// Only one instance may run at a time. Without this, closing the window hides
// to tray and launching the app again spawns another process, which breaks
// the auto-updater (NSIS can't close the old instances).
if (!app.requestSingleInstanceLock()) {
  app.quit();
} else {
  app.on('second-instance', () => {
    if (mainWindow) {
      if (mainWindow.isMinimized()) mainWindow.restore();
      mainWindow.show();
      mainWindow.focus();
    }
  });
}

let mainWindow = null;
let tray = null;
let isQuitting = false;
```

Edit 2: the install-update handler:
```js
  ipcMain.handle('app:install-update', () => {
    if (isDev) return;
    autoUpdater.quitAndInstall();
  });
```
→
```js
  ipcMain.handle('app:install-update', () => {
    if (isDev) return;
    isQuitting = true;
    // Spawn the installer first, then hard-exit so no Patel AutoPrint Admin
    // process is left running — otherwise NSIS shows "cannot be closed".
    autoUpdater.quitAndInstall(true, true);
    setTimeout(() => app.exit(0), 250);
  });
```

Wait — one thing: `autoUpdater.quitAndInstall(true, true)` → isSilent=true, isForceRunAfter=true. With isSilent=true and NSIS, the installer runs silently. Good. isForceRunAfter=true relaunches the app after install. Good.

But hmm — there's the `autoInstallOnAppQuit` interplay: quitAndInstall sets installOnQuit and quits; app.exit(0) bypasses quit events → onQuit handler won't fire → fine (already installed).

Actually wait, careful: `app.exit(0)` — will the detached installer spawned by `spawnLog` (which uses `detached: true`) survive? Yes.

But another subtlety: when app.exit(0) is called 250ms after quitAndInstall, the installer may not have STARTED yet (spawn happens in same tick, so process is created immediately). Even if the installer hasn't finished its UI init, it's a separate process; our exit doesn't affect it.

Also should I set `autoUpdater.autoInstallOnAppQuit = false` now that the button does hard-exit? No — keep it as a fallback for when user closes app normally. But note: with the button path, app.exit(0) bypasses onQuit, so autoInstallOnAppQuit won't double-install. And the normal close path (user closes app while update downloaded, no button click) → app quits properly → onQuit → installs silently. Good, keep both.

Now — I also want to double-check: is `app.exit` available? Yes, Electron app.exit().

Let me make the edits. Then bump version to 1.0.2, rebuild, ship, push.

Also should I update the package.json version first? Let me do edits, bump version, rebuild.

Actually — also reconsider `app.quit()` at top when lock fails: the `app.quit()` before ready. In Electron, calling app.quit() before ready is fine; whenReady won't fire. Good.

One more: in the else branch, `mainWindow` is declared with `let` AFTER this block — temporal dead zone is fine because the handler runs later (after mainWindow assigned). Good.

