All 6 images: perfectly centered, inside clip, full cell height (293.64), width 220.23 (portrait footprint as designed). Landscape images rotated 90°.

Also verify the OLD code failed this test? Not needed — we saw the bad stream.

Now also sanity-check 1-up path unaffected (it uses its own centering, lines 142-145, fine).

Also quickly test 2-up explicit landscape (user said that was perfect before) to ensure no regression: createContactSheet(files.slice(0,2), 2, 'A4', 't2', 'landscape'). The geometry differs: imageGrid(2) → 1×2 landscape. Could run quickly — let's add? Keep simple: run a variant inline. Actually cells for 2-up landscape: cols=1, rows=2 → cell420×297. Portrait 600×800: scale=min(416/600, 293/800)=0.366 → 220×293 — small but fit. Landscape: rotated footprint 220×293 as well? Hmm cellW=420.6, box416.6; landscape 800×600 rotated → effW=600: scale=min(416/600=0.69, 293/800=0.367)=0.367 → footprint 220×293 — centered in 420-wide cell. Fits, centered. Previously (v1.0.24 no rotation) landscape in 2-up: scale=min(416/800, 293/600)=min(0.52,0.488)=0.488 → 390×293 — bigger!

Interesting: user said 2-up explicit landscape was "perfect" — with rotation now, landscape images in 2-up become smaller (fit into portrait-ish cell220 wide instead of 390 wide). But cell is landscape-ish (420×293)... rotating a landscape image into a landscape cell makes it worse!

Wait — for 2-up landscape sheet: cellW=420.64, cellH=297.64 → cells are landscape-shaped. Rotating landscape photos90° → portrait footprint in landscape cell → smaller. That's bad for 2-up.

Hmm. The rotation idea was for 6-up (3×2) where cells are ~square-ish (280×297) — actually nearly square. Rotating a landscape photo (4:3) in a square cell: unrotated footprint = min-scaled: box276×293 → scale by width276/800=0.345 → 276×207 (landscape, 207 tall of 293 — leaves gaps top/bottom). Rotated: 220×293 (fills height, gaps left/right 28 each). Both leave gaps; rotated fills more area (220×293=64460 vs 276×207=57132). Marginally better.

For 2-up landscape cells (416×293): unrotated landscape photo: scale min(416/800=0.52, 293/600=0.488) → 390×293 fills height,390 of 416 wide — nearly full! Rotated: 220×293 — much smaller. So rotation hurts 2-up badly.

User said 2-up was perfect. If we rotate in 2-up, it becomes worse ("small size printing" complaint might partly be this!). Maybe user tested 2-up or 6-up.

So rotation should only apply when it helps: rotate only if the rotated fit scale > unrotated fit scale. I.e., compute both scales:
- uprightScale = min(boxW/imgW, boxH/imgH)
- rotatedScale = min(boxW/imgH, boxH/imgW)
- if rotatedScale > uprightScale → rotate.

For 6-up square-ish cell with4:3 landscape: upright = min(276/800=0.345, 293/600=0.488)=0.345; rotated = min(276/600=0.46, 293/800=0.367)=0.367. rotated > upright → rotate ✓ (slightly bigger).

For 2-up landscape cell: upright = min(416/800, 293/600)=0.488; rotated = min(416/600=0.69, 293/800=0.367)=0.367 < upright → don't rotate ✓ keeps "perfect" 2-up.

For portrait photo in square cell: upright min(276/600=0.46, 293/800=0.367)=0.367; rotated min(276/800=0.345,293/600=0.488)=0.345 < → don't rotate ✓ (portrait stays upright).

This "choose best fit" is elegant: always maximize coverage, no rule-of-thumb needed. And 2-up stays exactly as before.

